    <!-- UDFPS sensor location (x, y, radius) in native 1260x2800 px.
         Consumed for AIDL sensors via the metroid FingerprintProvider patch.
         Do NOT use config_sfps_sensor_props for this — SystemUI SideFps OverlapDetector
         crash-loops on it (verified 2026-07-10).

         Derivation (re-verified 2026-08-01, replacing the previous 640|2518|105):
         - The HAL really does report 1080x2400-space values. Read directly off
           IFingerprint::getSensorProps() (binder txn 1) on the running device:
           x=0x21c=540, y=0x880=2176, radius=0x5c=92.
         - This panel has NO 1080p mode — dumpsys display lists all three modes as
           1260x2800 — so the framework scale factor is always 1 and these coordinates
           must be supplied in native space. The override is mandatory, not cosmetic.
         - Native = HAL * 1260/1080 = HAL * 2800/2400 = HAL * 7/6:
           540*7/6 = 630, 2176*7/6 = 2538.7 -> 2539, 92*7/6 = 107.3 -> 107.
         - Corroboration for x=630: the HAL's 540 is exactly half of 1080, i.e. a
           horizontally centred sensor, and the display cutout spec centres the camera
           hole at x=630.5 on this panel. 630 is the true centre; 640 was 10 px right.
         - The previous value 640|2518|105 was attributed to
           persist.vendor.fingerprint.sensor_location, but that property is set nowhere:
           not on the device, not in /vendor/build.prop, and nowhere in the stock dump.
           Stock ships no config_udfps_sensor_props at all. So it had no traceable source.
         If fingerprint recognition regresses, revert to 640|2518|105 and re-test. -->
    <integer-array name="config_udfps_sensor_props" translatable="false">
        <item>630</item>
        <item>2539</item>
        <item>107</item>
    </integer-array>

    <!-- Default the panel to 120 Hz, matching stock Nothing OS.
         This is NOT cosmetic - it is required for the under-display fingerprint sensor.
         The panel driver refuses to enable local HBM (the bright flash that illuminates the
         finger for the optical sensor) unless the panel is already at 120 Hz:

           vendor/qcom/opensource/display-drivers/msm/sde/sde_connector.c
             if (fp_status && display->panel->cur_mode->timing.refresh_rate != 120) {
                     SDE_ERROR("fps not equal 120, wait!");
                     return 0;   // refuses, and never retries
             }

         Measured on the 20260802 build: idling at 60 Hz produced 2389 kernel
         "fps not equal 120" refusals, LHBM opening and closing again within 1-2 ms, and a
         fingerprint sensor that could not read (acquire stuck at 1, no enrollment possible).
         Forcing peak_refresh_rate=120 by hand made the panel run at 120.

         AOSP does vote for max refresh rate while UDFPS is active
         (DisplayModeDirector.UdfpsObserver -> Vote.PRIORITY_UDFPS, and
         config_ignoreUdfpsVote is already false), but at a 60 Hz idle the LHBM request
         races that vote and loses. Defaulting to 120 removes the race, as on stock. -->
    <integer name="config_defaultPeakRefreshRate">120</integer>

    <!-- config_defaultRefreshRate is deliberately NOT overridden here (AOSP default 60).
         An earlier revision pinned it to 120 to keep UDFPS local-HBM working, but that made
         120 Hz the *floor* and silently overrode Settings > Display > Smooth Display when a
         user turned it off. Replaced by the proper fix: UdfpsControllerOverlay.show() now
         requests the max refresh rate as soon as the fingerprint overlay appears (and hide()
         already drops it), so the panel is at 120 Hz before a finger lands and returns to the
         user's chosen rate afterwards. Peak stays 120 above so Smooth Display defaults on,
         matching stock. -->
</resources>
